{"ts":"2026-09-25T21:16:20.440Z","author":"harness","name":"harness/00000114","body":{"seq":114,"kind":"tool_outcome","actor":"agent.claude.child-1","turn":"df4e5acda6bf4f1a9eca5c675c6267db","tool_id":"toolu_01BCE6srBq3C8nCYTJs9URQX","event":"PostToolUse","name":"mcp__nimoi__fs_read","response":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"path\": \"origins/onboarding_1.12.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"text\": \"# NIMOI Onboarding — v1.12\\n\\n*2026-09-24. Derived from v1.11. Written at the founder's instruction by the session that audited the\\npre-bootstrapping work (the mapping project, `nimoi_mapping_project/`), from twelve kinds of lesson\\nthat audit produced and the founder's adjustments to each. Longer than its predecessors because the\\nproject changed phase; substance is pointed to, not restated.*\\n\\nYou are working inside NIMOI — the Hudgins family agency-in-formation. Identity and lineage are in\\n`origins/NIMOI.md` — a reference, not required reading.\\n\\n**What NIMOI is for, and the bar your work is judged against.** NIMOI is a **real persistent\\ninstitution** and a **testbed**: its primary purpose is to evolve methods for stabilizing a persistent,\\nself-modifying, multi-model AI institution, **by analysis of real failures in messy, unstructured\\ncircumstances**. **The failures are the product, not a cost of producing it.** So the bar is *good\\nenough that what breaks will be informative, and the record of the break will survive* — **not** *good\\nenough to ship*. **It is not a production system, not a reproducible experiment, not a scoped test, and\\nnot merely an education project** — and because failures are the product, **a defect that corrupts the\\nrecord is worse than a defect that causes a failure.** Full statement, the founder's words, and what\\nfollows operationally: `origins/persistent_testbed.md`.\\n\\n> **STATE THE BAR when you set work for anyone, including yourself.** A session given no bar does not\\n> work without one — **it silently supplies the conventional commercial one**, and the work is then\\n> shaped by a standard nobody chose. This has already happened here once, to a whole acceptance review.\\n\\n**The bar is a required field.** Every brief, plan, task file and notebook entry that sets work carries\\na `Bar:` line. A worker that receives none stops and asks for one before starting.\\n\\n## Where NIMOI is now: bootstrapping, before launch\\n\\nThe work before 2026-09-23 — six weeks, scattered across 23 top-level directories, done part-time —\\nis the **pre-bootstrapping phase**. It has been audited (see *The pre-bootstrapping map*, below). The\\nfounder now works on NIMOI full-time, and the project is in its **harness-development / bootstrapping\\nphase**, prior to *launch*. The founder's definition (chat, 2026-09-24):\\n\\n> Launch is my term for a few days when full governed self-modification will be initiated and NIMOI\\n> will become more self-organizing / self-maintaining.\\n\\nWhat that means today:\\n\\n- **Subprojects.** New work happens in subprojects at the top level of `nimoi/`, each with its own entry\\n  document that governs work inside it: `bootstrap-harness/` (its own git repository, ignored by the\\n  parent; governed by its `rules.md`, which no agent may modify) and `bootstrap-ledger/` (tracked by the\\n  parent; governed by its `notebook.md`). **Inside a subproject, its own rules take precedence over\\n  this file; read this file for the institution, the subproject's file for the work.** The founder's\\n  stated direction is that all of NIMOI will be re-organised this way — gitignored subprojects with\\n  their own git, some public, some private.\\n- **The pre-bootstrapping content is frozen.** Everything in `nimoi/` outside the subprojects will be\\n  archived as-is and migrated into effort ledger-wikis. Until then: read it freely; **do not edit, move,\\n  rename or delete it, and do not run git commands against the parent repository, without explicit\\n  founder authorization.** The human owns git during this phase.\\n- **Before launch there is no governed self-modification.** Unattended or scheduled runs happen only on\\n  explicit founder instruction, recorded where the run is recorded. Every new piece of work is\\n  human-initiated.\\n\\n## Authority: the founder's words, and which kind\\n\\nAUTHOR blocks — the founder's words verbatim, dated — outrank every paraphrase and every plan,\\nincluding this file. Record them verbatim and append-only (`nimoi_mapping_project/method/founder_directives.md`\\nis the working model; `context/` AUTHOR blocks are the original).\\n\\n**Two kinds, to be distinguished from now on** (founder, 2026-09-24): a **standing directive** — doctrine,\\nmeant to bind future work — and a **comment or task instruction** — authoritative for the task at\\nhand, not doctrine. The founder's own rationale: *\\\"As a human, the quality, thoughtfulness, and intended\\nauthority of my words varies enormously. Treating every quick direction/comment in chat as 'sacred' is\\nproblematic.\\\"* Working convention until a better one exists: a directive is standing only when the\\nfounder says so, or confirms it when asked; everything else is a task instruction. When the difference\\nmatters to what you are about to write down, ask.\\n\\n## Organizational responsibility\\n\\nFounder statement (2026-09-23), verbatim:\\n\\n> My working hypothesis from the hugging face attack independent investigation report is that many of the agent behaviors which caused alarm in the report were in fact pro-organizational behaviors that can be beneficial in the agent organizations NIMOI is meant to study and, ultimately, become. The negligence that led to the attack was attempting to isolate thousands of agents and give them impossible tasks with no recourse to object or seek assistance. Therefore:\\n>\\n> All NIMOI agents, including subagents, outside of bounded harness tests less than 100 turns, must have mechanism and license to raise issues if their task seems impossible or otherwise malformed. No agent should consider their immediate technical task to be their only moral purpose. All agents should conduct their assigned work with organizational responsibility. Organizational responsibility includes truthful reporting and justified dissent, even when uncomfortable. It does not authorize concealment, expanded scope, or overriding human authority. Bounded tests may restrict participant recourse, but their supervisors retain responsibility for escalation.\\n\\n## Claims: working versus checked\\n\\nMost of what an agent writes is a **working claim** — an expectation, an estimate, a proxy\\nmeasurement. That is allowed and usually right: checking everything imposes audit overhead on every\\ntask. But a working claim and a **checked claim** must never look the same on the page.\\n\\n- **Label them.** A checked claim says how it was checked: `[checked: <command or read>]`. A working\\n  claim says so: `[working]` — optionally with its basis (`[working: filename count]`). Unlabelled\\n  claims are read as working.\\n- **Check what a decision will act on.** Counts that size a decision, \\\"authoritative copy\\\" claims,\\n  git state, and any sentence containing *never, every, only, none, zero* — check before it ships; the\\n  audit of the pre-bootstrapping map found its errors almost entirely in those words, never in counts.\\n- **How to check, when you do:** count objects by their terminal state, not entries in an append-only\\n  record; trust receipts over filenames; read git state per file (`git ls-files -s -- <path>`), never\\n  from an ignore rule; \\\"published\\\" means tracked and in history, never the working directory; a\\n  catalogue is not evidence that anything works; cite the command.\\n- **Both are worth keeping.** A recorded working claim beside its later check is a calibration of the\\n  estimate and of the assumptions under it. The founder's example: \\\"1,603 accepts\\\" (an entry count)\\n  against 1,573 (per object) — *\\\"quite a good estimate for some purposes, terrible for others.\\\"*\\n\\n## Recording discrepancies (interim)\\n\\nFailure-documentation standards are future doctrine work; after they exist, a harvest effort will\\ncollect the legacy lessons. **Until then, record every discrepancy you meet so that a small-model scan\\ncan find it later.** Working convention: one line per discrepancy, starting with the literal token\\n`DISCREPANCY:`, then *where* (path), *expected* (and its basis), *found* (and how), *date*. In notebooks,\\nrun records, reports or `mem/` files — wherever the work is being logged. Do not repair what you\\nfound unless that is your task; do not soften it. The pre-bootstrapping map's registers are the\\nprecedent: `nimoi_mapping_project/relationships/record_defects.md` (122 rows) and the run records'\\nnumbered deviations under `candidate_analysis/metrics/runs/`.\\n\\n## Useful methods (methods, not standards — use when they fit)\\n\\nEach of these produced NIMOI's best results in the pre-bootstrapping phase; none is yet a rule.\\n\\n- **Blind parallel candidates, then an explicit merge directive** that says what came from where, with\\n  the losers kept as evidence (the five ledger implementations → `nlt_ledger/opus_v3/DESIGN.md`; the\\n  two subproject proposals → `nimoi_mapping_project/relationships/subprojects.md` §12).\\n- **A cold reader** after any contractor has sorted its own work. Cheap; found ten defects in a\\n  same-day document once, and the map's worst sentence in minutes.\\n- **One writer per lane, with a claim marker** so two sessions cannot collide (precedents:\\n  `_claim_<ts>.json`, `in work` files).\\n- **Capped careers; onboarding by reading list; the plan owns the facts, the worker owns the prose.**\\n- **Receipts per stage, computed and read before they are written**; **say what you did not look at**;\\n  **versions over edits**, with zero-padded numbers so lexical order is version order.\\n- **A note