n facing a difficult self-optimization problem, do not optimize for one variable. Instead,\\n> state a relevant virtue and the two vices that lie on either side, and then use that to guide your\\n> next steps.\\n\\n**The form is Aristotle's; the names are not.** Name each axis and both vices technically, for the\\nagency behaviour they describe. Rationale: `origins/virtue_methodology.md`. **Trajectories are tracked\\nin `origins/virtues.md`** — append-only, one section per axis, both vices, a *Watch* line per axis.\\nRead the relevant axis before deciding; append one line after. **Hard rules are not axes.** (Note: no\\ntracked file instructs the measurement runs that wrote most of the register's recent lines; the\\ninstruction lived in a scheduled-task prompt outside the repository.)\\n\\n## Working conventions\\n\\n- Read what the task needs, when it needs it; onboarding never requires a reading tour.\\n- Versions over edits, everywhere it matters. History is institutional capital; nothing is deleted.\\n- **Persistent tooling is written in Python.** Founder standard, 2026-08-23. A tool is the mechanism;\\n  any shell shown in prose is a convenience wrapper and never the check itself. Tools enumerate\\n  directories themselves rather than relying on shell globbing.\\n- Keep this file lean; background and rationale go in `context/` (write-don't-edit) or the\\n  subproject's `mem/`, with a pointer.\\n- Corrections from the founder are taken completely and without residue. Never optimize a single\\n  variable.\\n- **How onboarding is updated:** a new version file, next number up; the latest is authoritative;\\n  history is never edited or deleted; an agent may edit only a version it created in the current\\n  session while it is still the latest. **Naming — binding since 2026-08-17: `onboarding_<major>.<NN>.md`,\\n  minor always zero-padded**, so lexical order is version order and `ls origins/onboarding_*.md | sort | tail -1`\\n  is the latest. Never write an unpadded 